      "trace_id": "0a8af9da613707dfb75be33c590dcb05",
      "output": {
        "question": "What was the nickname of Mikael?",
        "answer": "Based on the passages from the books, Mikael Blomkvist has several nicknames:\n\n1. **Kalle Blomkvist** (or **Kalle Fucking Blomkvist**) - This is his most prominent nickname, given to him by reporters after he helped expose the Bear Gang bank robberies as a young journalist. He detested it because it was used tauntingly and reminded him of his youth [E1]. Salander also uses this derogatory form [E2].\n\n2. **Micke** - A shorter, more familiar nickname used by Berger [E3].\n\n3. **Master Detective Blomkvist** - Used sarcastically by Salander [E4].\n\n4. **Practical Pig** - A comparison to the character from \"The Three Little Pigs\" that Salander thinks of when considering whether he would help her [E2].\n\nThe most well-known and frequently referenced nickname is **Kalle Blomkvist**, which originated from his early journalistic career when he helped catch the Bear Gang. Despite never having used the name Carl in his life, this became his taunting moniker among peers and reporters [E1].\n\npassage_ids: [\"a0551c1d\", \"32d305f8\", \"00f2229c\", \"0455d349\"]",
        "citations": [
          "[E1]",
          "[E2]",
          "[E3]",
          "[E4]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_008.html",
            "paragraph": 12,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 1 Friday, December 20",
            "page": "7",
            "text": "It made no difference that Blomkvist had never in life used the name Carl. From that moment on, to his dismay, he was nicknamed Kalle Blomkvist by his peers—an epithet employed with taunting provocation, not unfriendly but not really friendly either. In spite of his respect for Astrid Lindgren—whose books he loved—he detested the nickname. It took him several years and far weightier journalistic successes before the nickname began to fade, but he still cringed if ever the name was used in his hearing.",
            "paragraph_end": 12,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "a0551c1ded8b286fd80fd17eab2eb6bee1f09636a882594e46695576958f0eff",
            "id": "a0551c1d-ed8b-286f-d80f-d17eab2eb6be",
            "agent_label": "E1"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_070.html",
            "paragraph": 30,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "136",
            "text": "Eriksson ran into Paolo Roberto in the stairwell to Millennium’s offices when she arrived at 8:30. She recognized him at once, introduced herself, and let him in. He had a bad limp. She smelled coffee and knew that Berger was already there.\n“Hello, Erika. Thanks for agreeing to see me at such short notice,” the boxer said.\nBerger studied the impressive collection of bruises and lumps on his face before she leaned forward and gave him a kiss on the cheek.",
            "paragraph_end": 32,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "32d305f8f0c29eab2599898468c1aa3d7ecaeaa98cc540b978799996f3e345e8",
            "id": "32d305f8-f0c2-9eab-2599-898468c1aa3d",
            "agent_label": "E2"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_042.html",
            "paragraph": 64,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "80",
            "text": "The events of the past few days have linked us again, whether you like it or not. The police are saying that you murdered two people I was very fond of. I was the one who discovered Dag and Mia minutes after they were shot. I don’t think it was you who shot them. I certainly hope it wasn’t. The police claim you’re a psychotic killer, but that would mean that I totally misjudged you or that you’ve changed dramatically over the past year. And if you’re not the murderer, then the police are chasing the wrong person.",
            "paragraph_end": 64,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "00f2229c35e7a82f13cf65586651171a83b23f2a823b610ab0b05b540ce5902e",
            "id": "00f2229c-35e7-a82f-13cf-65586651171a",
            "agent_label": "E3"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_009.html",
            "paragraph": 117,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 2 Friday, December 20",
            "page": "8",
            "text": "“Which means that he’s going to be cleaned out,” Salander said.\n“Is he honest?”\n“That’s his trust capital, so to speak. His image is to appear as the guardian of robust morality as opposed to the business world, and he is invited pretty regularly to pontificate on tele vision.”\n“There probably isn’t much left of that capital after his conviction today,” Frode said.\n“I don’t want to claim that I know exactly what demands are made on a journalist, but after this setback it will probably be a long time before Master Detective Blomkvist wins the Grand Prize for Journalism. He’s really made a fool of himself this time,” Salander said. “If I may make a personal comment . . . ”",
            "paragraph_end": 121,
            "part_end": 1,
